   $$

2. **Divide total participants by number of teams:**
   $$
   30 \text{ people} \div 6 \text{ teams} = 5 \text{ people per team}
   $$

This problem is a good example of the "dividing using the Distributive Property" section because it involves distributing a total quantity (30 people) evenly among a certain number of groups (6 teams). The Distributive Property is used here to split the total number into equal parts. The Distributive Property states that $ a \times (b + c) = a \times b + a \times c $, but in this context, we apply the concept of dividing a sum (23 + 7) by a number (6), which is similar to distributing the total to each team. This is analogous to distributing the total number of people across the teams equally.

Therefore, the number of people on each team is:
